      "math_academy_id": "e-3261",
      "problem": "Find $\\sec L$ for $\\triangle KLJ$ (shown below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224584/assets/c08613b38502628d71ffa0cdac65400420fb25926a64b747b925ee06d6d560ca.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that $\\sec$ is the reciprocal of $\\cos.$ So first we need to compute cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{JL}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $JL,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}JL & =\\sqrt{K{L}^{2}-J{K}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{JL}{5} = \\dfrac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡L & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224584/observations/step-e3261-3fa02fc1c380becae44b.html",
